Hard to tell which joint it's aligning to - oddly at some Y rotations, nothing seems to allow the ray to point more than a few degrees below horizontal, while when pointing in a different direction, it works almost as expected. This ray was set up originally for the controller, not the hand directly. Since v. 127 that ray/controller moves to the hand when the controller is set down, so that may affect its alignment. 

Here's my work-around for hiding unwanted hand rays altogether:

In XRHandModelFactory.js, just below:

```js
controller.addEventListener( 'connected', ( event ) => {
const xrInputSource = event.data;
```

add this:

```js
// hide rays 
if ( xrInputSource.hand) {
var ray1 = window.scene.getObjectByName('ray1');
if (ray1)
ray1.visible = false;
var ray2 = window.scene.getObjectByName('ray2');
if (ray2)
ray2.visible = false;
// stop ray click
window.isIndexTip1 = true;
}
```

The lines for the rays were named ray1 and ray2 when they were set up and added to the controllers. Also window.isIndexTip1 is used to prevent ray clicks, in this case by stopping my two ray action functions, rayClick and rayDown, for example:

```js
function rayClick(event, userId, threeJs) {
if(window.isIndexTip1)return false; ...
```

The rays are restored just under:

```js
controller.addEventListener( 'disconnected', () => {
```

with:

```js
// show rays
var ray1 = window.scene.getObjectByName('ray1');
if (ray1)
ray1.visible = true;
var ray2 = window.scene.getObjectByName('ray2');
if (ray2)
ray2.visible = true;
// re-allow ray click
window.isIndexTip1 = false;
```

This allows the rays to be visible and work when the controller is picked up, and hidden and not fire accidentally when the controller is set down and hands are being used. A more elegant solution would be to have a settable option to hide the hand rays, then find, hide and disable them without having to name them ahead of time or make special provision to stop actions after they've started. In my case, all the click, mouse down, selectstart and selectend actions are consolidated into the rayClick and rayDown functions, so they're relatively easy to block when needed. I'm sure someone can think of a better solution.
